        <div>
          <h2>Why More Reformer Traffic Means More Cleaning Demand</h2>
          <p>Reformer Pilates has stayed one of the most-booked boutique workouts, and fall is when new sign-ups and packed calendars collide. Every reformer that turns over between classes needs a wipe-down: the carriage, shoulder rests, footbar, straps, and handles. Across a full schedule, that's dozens of high-touch surfaces cleaned dozens of times a day.</p>
          <p>The real operational problem isn't only how <em>much</em> you clean — it's <strong>availability</strong>. When an instructor has ninety seconds to reset the room, they can't be hunting for a wipe container or finding it empty. Empty stations slow turnover, frustrate staff, and get noticed by members who expect a spotless reformer. A well-stocked station signals a professionally run studio; a crumpled retail pack signals the opposite.</p>
          <div class="wpx-cta-row">
            <a class="wpx-btn wpx-btn--primary" data-cro="pilates-blog-problem-cta"
               href="https://wipex.co/collections/pilates-studio-shop">Stock Up on Pilates Studio Wipes →</a>
